      "math_academy_id": "q-35489",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{16}=1$, find its $y$-intercepts.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-1 & =4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and $1+\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,1-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{5},0).$",
